From: fatalcoder524 <11532648+fatalcoder524@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] KernelSU-Next: start the usbadb_gate reader from KERNEL_SU_RC

The driver decides whether a PC is attached and publishes the verdict on
/dev/usbadb_gate; something init accepts has to act on it. KernelSU-Next already
has the plumbing: KERNEL_SU_RC is appended to init.rc through a read() proxy, so
init parses these triggers as its own and forks the child itself.

This adds one trigger to that string:

    on property:sys.boot_completed=1
        exec_background u:r:ksu:s0 root -- /system/bin/sh -c "<reader loop>"

Boot is a one-shot trigger with no ordering hazard: both the hardware and
post-fs-data triggers have fired by then, /data and /system are mounted, and the
reader's first read() is a request for a fresh line, so it gets the next verdict
whenever the cable state next changes. It also re-checks the current verdict on
start, so booting with the cable already plugged does not leave adb off.

exec_background, not exec: exec sets SVC_EXEC and leaves
Service::is_exec_service_running() true while the child lives, and init's main
loop skips command execution while that is set. This child never exits, so exec
would silently stop init running commands from every action.

The property trigger this replaces - on property:sys.oplus.usb.service_is_running -
could only work in one direction: that property is written by adbd's own service
machinery, so it can only become 1 while adbd is already running. Switching adb
off worked; switching it on could not. The trigger has to be independent of
adbd, which is what the gadget driver provides.

The script contains no double quote and no backslash: init's tokenizer copies
bytes verbatim inside "...", and C plus init together would need each backslash
escaped twice.

Signed-off-by: fatalcoder524 <11532648+fatalcoder524@users.noreply.github.com>
--- a/kernel/runtime/ksud_integration.c
+++ b/kernel/runtime/ksud_integration.c
@@ -48,6 +48,22 @@
     "on property:sys.boot_completed=1\n"
     "    exec u:r:" KERNEL_SU_DOMAIN ":s0 root -- " KSUD_PATH " boot-completed\n"
     "\n"
+    "on property:sys.boot_completed=1\n"
+    "    exec_background u:r:" KERNEL_SU_DOMAIN ":s0 root -- /system/bin/sh -c \""
+    "adb_on() { /system/bin/setprop sys.usb.state adb; /system/bin/setprop sys.usb.config "
+    "adb; /system/bin/setprop persist.sys.usb.config adb; /system/bin/start adbd; }; "
+    "adb_off() { /system/bin/setprop sys.usb.state mtp; /system/bin/setprop sys.usb.config "
+    "mtp; /system/bin/setprop persist.sys.usb.config mtp; /system/bin/stop adbd; }; sync() { "
+    "v=$$(sed -n 's/^adb=1.*/adb/p' /sys/kernel/usbadb_gate/link 2>/dev/null); case $$v in "
+    "adb) adb_on ;; *) adb_off ;; esac; }; say() { echo usbadb_gate: $$* >> "
+    "/data/local/tmp/usbadb_gate.log; echo usbadb_gate: $$* > /dev/kmsg 2>/dev/null; }; say "
+    "reader booting; [ -r /dev/usbadb_gate ] || { say cannot read /dev/usbadb_gate; exit 0; "
+    "}; exec 3< /dev/usbadb_gate; say reader started; sync; while :; do if read -r verdict "
+    "udc seq <&3; then case $$verdict in on) adb_on ;; off) adb_off ;; *) say unrecognised "
+    "verdict $$verdict ;; esac; elif [ ! -e /dev/usbadb_gate ]; then say device gone, "
+    "exiting; exit 0; elif [ $$SECONDS -ge $$((warn + 10)) ]; then warn=$$SECONDS; say read "
+    "keeps failing, check the driver; fi; done\"\n"
+    "\n"
     "\n";
 // clang-format on
 
